# FIXED DEFECT, 2026-08-15. Symptom: an `extern` group whose members are ALL
# records could not claim its own failure. A missing package gave
# `internal error: heroes-ffi-package …` at **exit 2** — the compiler blaming
# itself, on a program whose every line is correct, for a library that is simply
# not installed. Same for a missing header and a missing linker library.
#
# Cause: `emit/ffi.rs`'s `group_head` and `package_span`, and
# `emit/ffi_build.rs`'s `link_head`, all matched `Function` and `Constant` and
# dropped everything else. Those three are how a driver's marker gets attributed
# to a `.hero` line, so a group with no function had nothing to be attributed to.
#
# WHAT MAKES IT WORTH ITS OWN CASE. An audit commissioned during panel 062 found
# all three and ranked them "LOUD, but the compiler blames itself"; they were
# QUEUED rather than fixed, on the ground that a loud failure can wait. Two commits
# later they were the CI failure — and not because a reader met one, but because
# the *skip rule* that lets a runner without `pkg-config` pass reads
# `error[ffi_package]`, which is exactly the diagnostic these three could not
# produce. A defect that only blames the wrong party is still a defect that other
# machinery reads.
#
# The witness is a group of records only, deliberately: one function in it would
# have hidden all three.

extern "raylib.h" package "definitely-not-a-real-package-062"   #~v ffi_package
    record Color
        r: u8
        g: u8
        b: u8
        a: u8

function main()
    print(Color(r: 1, g: 2, b: 3, a: 4).r)
